1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,199
Turn this motor's shaft by hand, with
nothing connected,

2
00:00:04,239 --> 00:00:07,589
and it catches at 24 positions per
revolution.

3
00:00:07,629 --> 00:00:13,250
Feed it 20 A, timed to the rotor, and
Lesson 5's field solution

4
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:18,593
gives 0.71 N·m on average, swinging by
0.22 either way.

5
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:22,499
Both come from one function, the co-
energy. Today we take it apart:

6
00:00:22,539 --> 00:00:26,536
the steady torque, the ripple, and how to
remove most of it.

7
00:00:27,436 --> 00:00:29,715
First, the answers from Lesson 8.

8
00:00:29,755 --> 00:00:36,309
One: with L = L0 + kx, at constant
current, the force is ½ I² k.

9
00:00:36,349 --> 00:00:40,773
Two: with linear iron, double the current,
and the force grows four times.

10
00:00:40,813 --> 00:00:44,995
Three: the co-energy holds each current
times the magnet's flux linkage,

11
00:00:45,035 --> 00:00:48,174
so its slope, i dλm/dθ, is linear in
current.

12
00:00:49,024 --> 00:00:51,567
Here's the reference motor, solved in 2D:

13
00:00:51,607 --> 00:00:55,772
twelve tooth coils in three phases, and
eight surface magnets.

14
00:00:55,812 --> 00:00:58,939
With linear iron, its co-energy has three
parts.

15
00:00:58,979 --> 00:01:05,267
½ iᵀ L i, from the self and mutual
inductances; iᵀ λm(θ), the magnet flux

16
00:01:05,307 --> 00:01:11,722
linking each phase times its current; and
W′pm, from the magnets alone.

17
00:01:11,762 --> 00:01:15,332
Differentiate with respect to rotor angle
at constant current,

18
00:01:15,372 --> 00:01:19,657
and each part gives a torque: reluctance,
alignment, and cogging.

19
00:01:20,507 --> 00:01:24,679
Start with alignment. Take sinusoidal
linkages:

20
00:01:24,719 --> 00:01:30,176
λm cos θe for the first phase, the others
lagging by 120°.

21
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:34,048
The slope with mechanical angle brings out
p, the pole pairs.

22
00:01:34,088 --> 00:01:37,572
Put the current on the q axis, 90° ahead
of the magnet,

23
00:01:37,612 --> 00:01:41,588
and each phase current follows the same
sine as its slope.

24
00:01:41,628 --> 00:01:45,645
Each phase's torque pulses as sine
squared, but the three pulses,

25
00:01:45,685 --> 00:01:49,565
a third of a cycle apart, add to a
constant: three halves.

26
00:01:49,605 --> 00:01:56,848
So T = 3/2 p λm i_q. Current on the d axis
adds nothing: its terms cancel.

27
00:01:57,699 --> 00:02:05,408
Energy gives a second route. Each back-EMF
is ωm times the slope of its linkage,

28
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:10,163
so the power, Σ e·i, is ωm times the same
sum.

29
00:02:10,203 --> 00:02:13,833
Power is torque times speed, so the torque
is that sum again.

30
00:02:13,873 --> 00:02:20,696
At 3000 rpm and 20 A on q, the EMF peak is
8.17 V:

31
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:28,820
3/2 × 8.17 × 20 = 245 W. Divided by 314
rad/s: 0.780 N·m.

32
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:32,356
That's the torque constant, Kt = 3/2 p λm:

33
00:02:32,396 --> 00:02:36,600
0.039 N·m per amp, with the nominal 6.5
mWb.

34
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:42,596
Now the solver. Hold 20 A, and swing the
current away from the magnet's d axis,

35
00:02:42,636 --> 00:02:46,047
averaging over one cogging period at each
angle.

36
00:02:46,087 --> 00:02:49,095
The points fall on a sine: zero on d,

37
00:02:49,135 --> 00:02:53,267
a peak at 90°, all on q, and braking past
180°.

38
00:02:53,307 --> 00:03:01,578
The peak is 0.713 N·m, not 0.78: the 2D
flux linkage is 5.94 mWb,

39
00:03:01,618 --> 00:03:08,782
8.6% low, with no end flux and an assumed
Br of 1.2 T.

40
00:03:08,822 --> 00:03:12,620
A sin 2γ term would mean reluctance
torque.

41
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:18,338
It's under 0.01%: in a surface-magnet
motor, Ld equals Lq.

42
00:03:19,188 --> 00:03:23,543
Against q current, the torque is a
straight line,

43
00:03:23,583 --> 00:03:27,431
0.0357 N·m per amp, all the way to 60 A.

44
00:03:27,471 --> 00:03:30,294
That's built in: this iron is linear.

45
00:03:30,334 --> 00:03:35,159
Real steel bends past its knee, about 1.5
T, from Lesson 4.

46
00:03:35,199 --> 00:03:39,491
The magnets alone put 1.49 T through the
teeth,

47
00:03:39,531 --> 00:03:45,413
and current on q adds little: 1.52 T at 20
A, 1.59 T at 40 A.

48
00:03:45,453 --> 00:03:49,871
So expect the real torque per amp to droop
gently beyond rated current.

49
00:03:49,911 --> 00:03:54,367
By how much needs a nonlinear solution,
which this model isn't.

50
00:03:55,217 --> 00:03:58,264
Nor is the flux linkage a pure sine.

51
00:03:58,304 --> 00:04:04,909
The solver's back-EMF has a fifth harmonic
of 8.5%, and a seventh of 1.9%.

52
00:04:04,949 --> 00:04:08,547
With sinusoidal currents, both make torque
at six times

53
00:04:08,587 --> 00:04:12,749
the electrical frequency: once every 15
mechanical degrees.

54
00:04:12,789 --> 00:04:18,087
Its size is the seventh minus the fifth,
with their signs,

55
00:04:18,127 --> 00:04:23,818
over the fundamental: ±6.5% of the mean,
0.046 N·m at 20 A.

56
00:04:23,858 --> 00:04:27,743
Lesson 6's ideal flat-topped field would
give 8.1%;

57
00:04:27,783 --> 00:04:31,208
the solver's softer magnet edges help.

58
00:04:32,058 --> 00:04:37,618
Now switch the current off. The solver
still finds torque:

59
00:04:37,658 --> 00:04:41,491
±0.219 N·m, 24 cycles per revolution.

60
00:04:41,531 --> 00:04:45,174
Field lines from each magnet edge lean
toward the nearest tooth

61
00:04:45,214 --> 00:04:49,156
corner and pull on it. Over a cycle, the
pulls average to zero.

62
00:04:49,196 --> 00:04:51,226
That's 31% of the torque at 20 A:

63
00:04:51,266 --> 00:04:54,475
the biggest part of the ripple we started
with.

64
00:04:54,515 --> 00:04:58,766
Subtract it from the loaded torque, and
the rest matches the

65
00:04:58,806 --> 00:05:03,382
alignment term within 0.02%: the
reluctance term is tiny.

66
00:05:04,232 --> 00:05:09,257
Why 24? Each magnet alone would cog with
the slot pitch, 30 degrees.

67
00:05:09,297 --> 00:05:13,013
With 12 slots and 8 poles, neighbouring
magnets sit half a slot

68
00:05:13,053 --> 00:05:16,887
pitch apart relative to the teeth: their
slot-pitch parts cancel,

69
00:05:16,927 --> 00:05:19,870
and their half-pitch parts add, in all
eight.

70
00:05:19,910 --> 00:05:24,054
That's 24 per turn: the least common
multiple of 12 and 8.

71
00:05:24,094 --> 00:05:27,633
With 10 poles, the magnets spread over
five positions,

72
00:05:27,673 --> 00:05:32,704
so only every fifth part survives: 60 per
turn, and usually much weaker.

73
00:05:32,744 --> 00:05:37,208
Zhu and Howe showed that a large LCM,
relative to slots times poles,

74
00:05:37,248 --> 00:05:41,722
tends to mean low cogging. Our wide slot
openings add to it.

75
00:05:42,572 --> 00:05:45,921
The standard fix is skew: twist the slots
along the stack,

76
00:05:45,961 --> 00:05:50,370
or step the magnets, so each slice sees
the teeth at a different angle.

77
00:05:50,410 --> 00:05:55,475
In a slice model, torque is the 2D curve
averaged over the skew. Skew by

78
00:05:55,515 --> 00:06:01,164
one cogging period, 15 degrees, and every
cogging harmonic averages to zero.

79
00:06:01,204 --> 00:06:09,945
Half a period leaves ±0.126 N·m; three
magnet segments, 5° apart, leave ±0.014.

80
00:06:09,985 --> 00:06:15,782
The cost: the skew factor, 0.955, trims
the torque by 4.5%,

81
00:06:15,822 --> 00:06:21,425
and the sixth-harmonic ripple drops from
6.5% to 2.0%.

82
00:06:21,465 --> 00:06:26,067
Real skewed motors keep some cogging from
tolerances and end effects.

83
00:06:26,917 --> 00:06:29,050
Other fixes, in one line each.

84
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:32,408
Shape the magnets, tuning their arc or
thinning their edges,

85
00:06:32,448 --> 00:06:35,562
so the field changes gently past each
tooth corner.

86
00:06:35,602 --> 00:06:39,614
Choose slots and poles with a large LCM,
like 12 and 10.

87
00:06:39,654 --> 00:06:43,585
Or notch the tooth faces: the rotor sees
more, smaller openings,

88
00:06:43,625 --> 00:06:47,599
and cogging moves to a higher, usually
weaker, harmonic.

89
00:06:48,449 --> 00:06:51,097
To measure cogging, leave the windings
open,

90
00:06:51,137 --> 00:06:55,369
and clamp the motor body in an indexing
head, or on a protractor.

91
00:06:55,409 --> 00:06:59,508
Fix a 100 mm arm to the shaft, resting on
a kitchen scale.

92
00:06:59,548 --> 00:07:03,733
Step the body by half a degree: 30
readings per period.

93
00:07:03,773 --> 00:07:08,126
Hang a 300 g weight at the tip so it never
lifts, and subtract the

94
00:07:08,166 --> 00:07:13,019
average over a period. Torque is the
reading, times g, times the arm.

95
00:07:13,059 --> 00:07:17,912
This model predicts ±2.19 N at the tip,
±223 g.

96
00:07:17,952 --> 00:07:22,584
Bearing friction is only about 1% of that.

97
00:07:22,624 --> 00:07:25,530
Expect less on a real motor: many are
skewed,

98
00:07:25,570 --> 00:07:28,492
and this 2D model has no end effects.

99
00:07:29,342 --> 00:07:31,622
Three predictions before next time.

100
00:07:31,662 --> 00:07:34,626
One: how many cogging periods per
revolution

101
00:07:34,666 --> 00:07:38,102
does a motor with 9 slots and 6 poles
have?

102
00:07:39,002 --> 00:07:44,175
Two: the reference motor, with 15 A peak
on the q axis. What torque?

103
00:07:45,075 --> 00:07:50,213
Three: if Ld and Lq differ, can a negative
d-axis current add torque?

104
00:07:51,113 --> 00:07:55,650
Next time: saliency, where the reluctance
term stops averaging out.
